# Interactive Text Components Implementation Guide

## Overview

When implementing text components with clickable links (like Terms of Service, Privacy Policy, etc.), follow these patterns for consistency and accessibility.

## Core Implementation Pattern

### 1. Locale String Structure

Break interactive text into semantic parts for flexibility:

```typescript
// In locale files (e.g., src/locale/en.ts)
auth: {
  termsPrefix: 'I agree to the ',
  termsOfServiceLink: 'Terms of Service',
  termsMiddle: ' & ',
  privacyPolicyLink: 'Privacy Policy',
}
```

### 2. Interactive Text Component

Create a dedicated component for complex interactive text:

```tsx
const InteractiveText: React.FC = () => {
  const handleLinkPress = useCallback((url: string, fallbackMessage: string) => {
    Linking.openURL(url).catch(() => {
      Alert.alert('Error', fallbackMessage)
    })
  }, [])

  return (
    <View style={styles.textContainer}>
      <Text style={styles.normalText}>
        {getString('prefix')}
        <Text style={styles.linkText} onPress={() => handleLinkPress(url1, {getString('error')})}>
          {getString('linkText1')}
        </Text>
        <Text style={styles.normalText}>{getString('middle')}</Text>
        <Text style={styles.linkText} onPress={() => handleLinkPress(url2, {getString('error')})}>
          {getString('linkText2')}
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </View>
  )
}
```

## Internationalization (i18n) Requirements

### Required import

```typescript
import { getString } from '@/locale/I18nConfig'
```

### Add all required strings in all locale files [src/locale/](mdc:src/locale)
```typescript
// In locale files (e.g., src/locale/en.ts)
auth: {
  termsPrefix: 'I agree to the ',
  termsOfServiceLink: 'Terms of Service',
  termsMiddle: ' & ',
  privacyPolicyLink: 'Privacy Policy',
}
```

### All Text Must Use getString()

Every user-facing text string must use the `getString()` function for internationalization support.

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Localized text
title={getString('gift.title')}
buttonText={getString('gift.redeemNow')}
errorMessage={getString('errors.networkError')}

// ❌ INCORRECT - Hardcoded strings
title="Gift"
buttonText="Redeem Now"
errorMessage="Network error occurred"
```

### Key Structure Convention

Use consistent, hierarchical key naming with dot notation:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Hierarchical structure
getString('feature.section.item')
getString('gift.category.all')
getString('auth.validation.passwordRequired')
getString('errors.api.networkTimeout')

// ❌ INCORRECT - Flat or inconsistent naming
getString('giftCategoryAll')
getString('PASSWORD_REQUIRED')
getString('network-timeout-error')
```

### Dynamic Content with Interpolation

Use `getString()` with interpolation for dynamic content:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - String interpolation
getString('gift.pointsRequired', { points: 150 })
getString('user.welcomeMessage', { name: user.name })
getString('validation.minLength', { min: 8 })

// Locale string examples:
// "gift.pointsRequired": "{{points}} points required"
// "user.welcomeMessage": "Welcome back, {{name}}!"
// "validation.minLength": "Minimum {{min}} characters required"
```

### Interactive Text Localization

For components with clickable links, localize all text parts:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Localized interactive text
const TermsText: React.FC = () => (
  <Text style={styles.normalText}>
    {getString('auth.termsPrefix')}
    <Text style={styles.linkText} onPress={openTerms}>
      {getString('auth.termsOfServiceLink')}
    </Text>
    {getString('auth.termsMiddle')}
    <Text style={styles.linkText} onPress={openPrivacy}>
      {getString('auth.privacyPolicyLink')}
    </Text>
  </Text>
)

// Corresponding locale keys:
// "auth.termsPrefix": "I agree to the "
// "auth.termsOfServiceLink": "Terms of Service"
// "auth.termsMiddle": " & "
// "auth.privacyPolicyLink": "Privacy Policy"
```

### Error Messages and Feedback

Localize all user feedback messages:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Localized error handling
const handleError = (error: ApiError) => {
  const errorMessage = error.code 
    ? getString(`errors.api.${error.code}`)
    : getString('errors.general.unknown')
  
  showToast({
    type: TOAST_TYPE.ERROR,
    message: errorMessage
  })
}

// ✅ CORRECT - Localized success messages
const handleSuccess = () => {
  showToast({
    type: TOAST_TYPE.SUCCESS,
    message: getString('gift.redeemSuccess')
  })
}
```

### Form Validation Messages

All form validation must use localized strings:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Localized validation
const validationSchema = yup.object({
  email: yup
    .string()
    .email(getString('validation.email.invalid'))
    .required(getString('validation.email.required')),
  password: yup
    .string()
    .min(8, getString('validation.password.minLength', { min: 8 }))
    .required(getString('validation.password.required'))
})
```

### Placeholder and Label Text

Localize all input placeholders, labels, and helper text:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Localized form components
<FormInput
  name="email"
  label={getString('form.email.label')}
  placeholder={getString('form.email.placeholder')}
  helperText={getString('form.email.helper')}
/>

<FormDatePicker
  name="birthDate"
  label={getString('form.birthDate.label')}
  placeholder={getString('form.birthDate.placeholder')}
/>
```

### Best Practices

1. **No Hardcoded Strings**: Never use string literals for user-facing text
2. **Consistent Keys**: Use `feature.section.item` naming convention
3. **Group Related**: Group related strings under common prefixes
4. **Interpolation**: Use parameter substitution for dynamic content
5. **Fallbacks**: Provide fallback text for missing translations
6. **Context**: Include context in key names when meaning could be ambiguous

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT - Context-aware keys
getString('button.save.document')    // Save button in document context
getString('button.save.profile')     // Save button in profile context
getString('status.loading.data')     // Loading status for data
getString('status.loading.image')    // Loading status for image
```

